<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05K4">Corollary <span data-tag="05K4">4.7.9.23</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $n$ be an integer, and let $X$ be an $(n-1)$-truncated object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Then the canonical map $\operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} )}} \rightarrow \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}_{/X}$ is an equivalence of $\infty $-categories. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Combining Proposition <a data-tag="05K2" href="/tag/05K2">4.7.9.22</a> with Corollary <a data-tag="06RW" href="/tag/06RW">4.7.9.21</a>, we deduce that the canonical map $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \rightarrow \operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}_{/X}$ induces an isomorphism of homotopy $n$-categories. The desired result now follows from the observation that $\operatorname {h}_{\mathit{\leq {}n}}{\mathit{(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})}}_{/X}$ is an $n$-category (Corollary <a data-tag="06RV" href="/tag/06RV">4.7.7.24</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
